{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":1149312,"items":[{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,28]],"date-time":"2026-02-28T02:06:40Z","timestamp":1772244400696,"version":"3.50.1"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2023,6,15]]},"group-title":"Mathematics","reference-count":0,"publisher":"Cambridge University Press (CUP)","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2023,6,15]],"date-time":"2023-06-15T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1686787200000},"content-version":"unspecified","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/www.cambridge.org\/engage\/coe\/legal-information?show=terms-of-use"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"accepted":{"date-parts":[[2023,6,13]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Every positive integer $l \\in \\mathbb{N}$ can be formed $l = (m + n)d$, provided $gcd(m,n)=1$. From this point of view, the next formulas $n=\\sum_{d|l} \\varphi(d)$ and $\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=\\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\varphi(k)[\\frac{n}{k}]$, and these equivalence had been proved. In this paper on an extension of these results, the next formula is proved: $\\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\sum_{\\substack{(a+b)c=k \\\\ gcd(a,b)=1}} f(a,b)\\cdot g(c) = \\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\sum_{\\substack{a+b=k \\\\ gcd(a,b)=1}} f(a,b) \\sum_{i\\leq [\\frac{n}{k}]} g(i) = \\sum_{a+b \\leq n} f(\\frac{a}{gcd(a,b)},\\frac{b}{gcd(a,b)})\\cdot g(gcd(a,b))$. As corollaries, we have $\\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\tau(k)=\\sum_{k=1}^{n} [\\frac{n}{k}] = \\sum_{a+b \\leq n} \\frac{1}{\\varphi(\\frac{a+b}{gcd(a,b)})}$, $\\tau(n)=\\sum_{a+b = n} \\frac{1}{\\varphi(\\frac{a+b}{gcd(a,b)})}$, $\\sum_{\\substack{a+b=n \\\\ gcd(a,b)=1}} gcd(a-1,b+1) = \\sum_{a+b=n} \\frac{\\varphi(n)}{\\varphi(\\frac{n}{gcd(a,b)})}$, and so on. In addition to it, we evaluate a sequence $\\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\varphi(k)\\tau(k)$.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.33774\/coe-2023-sjwkq","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2023,6,15]],"date-time":"2023-06-15T05:15:45Z","timestamp":1686806145000},"source":"Crossref","is-reference